"""One-time, anchor-checked manuscript revision; retained as an editing record."""
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
path = ROOT / 'docs/manuscript/main.tex'
t = path.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig')

def paragraph(start, replacement):
    global t
    a = t.index(start)
    b = t.find('\n\n', a)
    assert b >= 0, start
    t = t[:a] + replacement + t[b:]

def span(start, end, replacement):
    global t
    a = t.index(start)
    b = t.index(end, a)
    t = t[:a] + replacement + '\n\n' + t[b:]

def exact(old, new):
    global t
    old = old.replace(chr(92)+chr(39), chr(39))
    assert old in t, old[:100]
    t = t.replace(old, new)

paragraph(r'\abstract{', r'''\abstract{Protecting drinking water sources requires timely information about conditions beyond directly monitored locations. This study presents a digital twin prototype integrating networked environmental observations, an established three-dimensional hydrodynamic water quality model, and source-protection applications at the Brusdalsvatnet reservoir in Norway. The contribution is the systems integration and the applications it enables; the governing equations and numerical solver are established methods. The framework connects state estimation for operational monitoring with contamination-scenario analysis and model-informed planning of uncrewed surface vessel sampling. Comparison of the revised thermal simulation with hourly observations at 2.95~m depth over the 2024 season gives a root-mean-square error of \texorpdfstring{1.73~$^{\circ}$C}{1.73 degrees Celsius} and a Pearson correlation of 0.962; mean depth-band RMSE is \texorpdfstring{2.15~$^{\circ}$C}{2.15 degrees Celsius}, with larger errors through the metalimnion. The contaminant scenario and sampling route demonstrate analytical capabilities but have not been validated in the field. Sensor retrieval is automated, while the complete operational update loop remains a proposed integration of implemented components. The results establish a proof of concept for source-water decision support and identify the measurements and operational testing needed before reliance on its predictions.}''')
paragraph('A digital twin is a model', '''A digital twin combines a representation of a physical system with observations that update knowledge of its state and support analysis and decisions. Applied to source-water protection, this framework can connect routine monitoring with spatial predictions and incident-response planning, particularly for constituents that cannot be monitored continuously.''')
paragraph('In this study, the authors investigated', r'''This study develops and demonstrates a systems-integration prototype for the Brusdalsvatnet drinking water reservoir in Norway. It builds on the sensing infrastructure and dashboard presented in \citep{primeau2024towards} by adding a three-dimensional modelling layer and applications for state estimation, contaminant-release analysis and model-informed mobile sampling. The contribution is the integration of these components in a source-protection workflow. The governing equations, discretization and solver are established methods; their development is not a contribution of this work.''')
paragraph('An earlier version of this work was posted', r'''An earlier version of this study was posted as a preprint \citep{primeau2025preprint}. The present article supersedes that version and presents the revised thermal evaluation, expanded model and sensor documentation, and explicit distinctions between demonstrated capabilities and proposed operational use.''')
paragraph('Water quality encompasses physical', r'''Water quality encompasses physical, chemical and biological properties that vary through a water body under the influence of sources, transport and reactions \citep{chapman2021water}. Some properties can be measured in situ using optical or electrochemical sensors, whereas many micropollutants and pathogenic organisms require specialized instruments or laboratory analysis \citep{zulkifli2018detection,Chidiac2023APerspectives}. Their spatial distributions are therefore generally less well observed than routine sensor variables such as temperature.''')
span('Modeling is another alternative', r'\subsection{Digital Twins}', r'''Process-based hydrodynamic water quality models (HWQM) represent circulation and constituent transport using conservation laws coupled to reaction formulations. Their capabilities and limitations have been studied extensively \citep{de2020historical,tsakiris2012water,Piccolroaz2024,Hipsey2020assessment}. This paper uses that established modelling approach as a component of the integrated system, rather than attempting to establish its general validity.

A process-based model provides an explicit, inspectable account of transport and reactions when observations of target constituents are sparse. Data-driven surrogates can also reproduce spatial water quality fields \citep{wang2025spatio,Mohammed2022EmulatingLearning}; the choice here reflects the available observations and the need to examine hypothetical releases through their constituent processes. It does not imply that physical models are universally more accurate or remove dependence on uncertain boundary conditions and parameters.

Three-dimensional resolution preserves variation in both horizontal directions and depth, supporting comparisons between sampling locations and the examination of subsurface plume pathways. One-dimensional models can reproduce vertical stratification, and laterally averaged two-dimensional models can represent depth-dependent transport; the required spatial detail depends on the application \citep{3D_better,Piccolroaz2024}. The three-dimensional representation was selected to support the spatial analyses in Section~\ref{ch:Experiments}. Its site-specific performance is assessed separately from the established numerical method.''')
paragraph('The essential feature of a digital twin', r'''Digital twin frameworks connect observations of a physical system with an updated virtual representation and functions for monitoring, prediction and analysis \citep{tao2018digital,barricelli2019survey}. For source-water protection, these functions correspond to estimating present conditions, exploring contamination scenarios and planning monitoring actions. An online dashboard provides an interface through which water managers can inspect observations and model results.''')
paragraph('Digital twins were originally conceived', r'''Digital twins are used across engineered and natural systems, with communication requirements and useful update intervals determined by the application \citep{alam2015design,verdouw2021digital}. In the water sector they support treatment and distribution operations \citep{MA2025108316,ConejosFuertes13092020}. For environmental monitoring, the one-to-three-hour latency described by Murphy et al. \citep{Murphy2015} provides a practical near-real-time target; whether an integrated modelling system meets it requires measurement of the full observation-to-publication interval.''')
