paragraph('The process models module', r'''The process-model module in Figure~\ref{fig:IO} uses the Delft3D FM hydrodynamic and water quality solvers. The following equations summarize the standard hydrostatic formulation; discretization, closure and numerical solution are documented by Deltares \citep{D-WAQ_manual,de2020historical}. For an incompressible flow, constituent concentration $C_n$ obeys the advection--diffusion--reaction balance''')
exact(r'D_x \frac{\partial^2 C_n}{\partial x^2}-u \frac{\partial C_n}{\partial x}+D_y \frac{\partial^2 C_n}{\partial y^2}-v \frac{\partial C_n}{\partial y} +D_z \frac{\partial^2 C_n}{\partial z^2}-w \frac{\partial C_n}{\partial z}+\sum S(t,...)', r'\nabla\!\cdot(\mathbf{D}\nabla C_n)-\mathbf{u}\!\cdot\nabla C_n+\sum S_n')
paragraph(r'\noindent where $C_n$', r'''\noindent where $C_n$ has units of mass per volume, $\mathbf{u}=(u,v,w)$ is velocity, $\mathbf{D}$ is the dispersion tensor, and $S_n$ denotes source and sink rates per unit volume. The divergence form allows spatially varying dispersion coefficients. Heat transport uses the corresponding energy balance with surface heat-flux terms.''')
exact(r'+\frac{\partial \bar{u} h}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial \bar{v} h}{\partial y}', r'-\frac{\partial (\bar{u} h)}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial (\bar{v} h)}{\partial y}')
exact(r'$F_x$, $F_y$ are Reynolds stresses with dimensions of force; $M_x$, $M_y$ are external sources and sinks of momentum, such as interactions with structures, inflows or outflows.', r'$F_x$, $F_y$ represent horizontal stress-divergence contributions per unit mass; $M_x$, $M_y$ represent other momentum-source contributions. Each has dimensions of acceleration.')
paragraph('Using the shallow water assumption', r'''Under the hydrostatic approximation, vertical acceleration is neglected in the momentum balance. For incompressible flow away from volumetric sources, continuity gives''')
paragraph('Equations~\\ref{Eq:momentumx} and', r'''The momentum equations use Reynolds averaging, with unresolved turbulent transport represented by closure and eddy coefficients. The supplied thermal configuration uses the $k$-$\epsilon$ closure; its settings are given in Appendix~\ref{app:modelconfig}.''')
paragraph('Finally, it follows from the shallow water assumption', r'''The hydrostatic pressure relationship is''')
paragraph('Equations \\ref{Eq:advection-diffusion}', r'''Surface heat exchange, wind stress, bed friction, inflows, outflows and constituent reactions complete the forcing of this system. These established processes are configured for the case study rather than introduced as new numerical methods.''')
span('In principle it is possible to use water quality data', 'The model scripts are managed', r'''The first profiler observation informs the initial temperature field. Subsequent profiles are used to compare simulated and observed conditions rather than directly prescribing temperatures in interior cells. Figure~\ref{fig:IO} makes this distinction explicit.

Figure~\ref{fig:dataflow} shows the intended operational architecture. Newly retrieved observations would update model boundary conditions; model output would supply a spatial state estimate and the initial conditions for a later update. Alternative configurations could be compared against available observations before selecting an output for publication. This proposed operational ensemble is distinct from both the historical configuration comparison in Figure~\ref{fig:meshconverge} and the three reaction alternatives used for sampling-plan analysis.

The implemented components support data retrieval, input preparation, model execution, evaluation and display, but the full sequence has not been demonstrated as an unattended hourly loop. Scenario analysis and sampling-plan generation are demonstrated offline. Appendix~\ref{app:workflow} distinguishes implemented components from proposed automation and reports sensor coverage and measured solver throughput.''')
paragraph('The model scripts are managed', r'''Python scripts and Windows Task Scheduler support the implemented workflow. A local application provides access to model preparation and analysis, while the public dashboard described in \citep{primeau2024towards} displays monitoring data and selected outputs. Table~\ref{tab:computer} reports the computing environment for the supplied thermal-run throughput benchmark.''')
span('An automated workflow retrieves data', 'In this study, the Delft3D FM suite', r'''The acquisition scripts retrieve surface-sonde and weather observations hourly and vertical profiles twice daily. Offline comparison against the profiler record provides a check on the resulting thermal field. Evaluating both stratified and cooling periods exposes errors that may be less apparent over a short spring window; it does not by itself identify the process responsible for a discrepancy.''')
paragraph('In this study, the Delft3D FM suite', r'''Delft3D FM was selected for its established hydrodynamic and water quality capabilities and prior applications to seasonally stratified lakes \citep{Delft_Validation1}. Solver settings for the revised thermal run are recorded in Appendix~\ref{app:modelconfig}. The case study covers the open-water season: ice processes are not represented in this configuration, and the profiler is removed during winter to prevent damage.''')
exact(r'Operational model update loop, annotated with the rate at which each step runs: retrieval at each platform\'s publication interval, an hourly model run costing on the order of one second of computation per simulated hour, and a nowcast published less than two hours after the observations behind it. Two paths close the loop: model output becomes the next update\'s initial condition, and a planned sampling mission is carried to the vessel manually. Measured coverage and latency are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:workflow}.', r'Proposed operational update loop assembled from the prototype components. Surface-sonde and weather retrieval are hourly and vertical profiles are retrieved twice daily; vessel data transfer is manual. Model restart, selection and publication illustrate the intended integration rather than a demonstrated unattended loop. Implementation status, data coverage and solver throughput are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:workflow}.')
paragraph('As shown in Figure~\\ref{fig:dataflow}, the function', r'''The fixed profiler samples a single horizontal location. Mobile profiles can instead target locations where alternative model predictions differ, providing observations with which to compare those alternatives. Because each mission has limited time for transit and profiling, selecting a small set of such locations is a useful planning task.''')
