span('Physical features of Brusdalsvatnet', 'Previous assessment has identified', r'''Physical features, sensing locations and the five scenario-analysis sites are mapped in Figure~\ref{fig:map}. Hydrological boundary conditions are externally supplied time series of tributary discharge, outlet flow and water abstraction, applied at eight tributaries and the two principal withdrawals. Their generation is outside the scope of this integration study. The revised thermal run uses time-varying supplied forcing; the earlier scenario outputs were generated with an earlier model and forcing configuration.

Third-party lake-level and outflow records contribute to the provenance of the supplied hydrological inputs and are not distributed with the public analysis package. Comparison with the available water-level observations gives RMSE 0.027~m and Pearson correlation 0.987 over 3,180 paired values, after aligning the level datum. These aggregate indicators describe agreement of the supplied model output with the monitoring record; the availability limits are stated in the Data Availability Statement.''')
paragraph('Figure~\\ref{fig:meshconverge} presents', r'''Figure~\ref{fig:meshconverge} compares accuracy and cost in the historical configuration campaign. Its vertical axis is the mean of depth-wise temperature RMSE values; the horizontal axis is the dimensionless ratio of simulated time to wall-clock time. The plotted runs share the 25 April--20 November 2024 period. Shorter runs are excluded to avoid comparing errors accumulated over different seasonal windows. These serial runs were performed on a separate workstation, so their throughput is not directly comparable with the partitioned thermal-run benchmark in Appendix~\ref{app:workflow}. Colour denotes Pearson correlation. The vertical line shows the range across configurations sharing a mesh and layer count, not a sampling confidence interval.''')
paragraph('Two results follow.', r'''At 40 layers, increasing the number of 3D cells from 36,197 to 65,900 changes RMSE from 1.336 to 1.325~$^{\circ}$C for a 1.6-fold increase in runtime. At fixed resolution, changing other settings spans RMSE values from 1.076 to 2.852~$^{\circ}$C. Thus, for this observation-based comparison, physical settings have a larger effect than the tested refinement. This is a limited resolution/configuration comparison, not proof of grid-independent transport. The revised thermal configuration evaluated below was supplied separately and is not one of the plotted candidates; Figure~\ref{fig:meshconverge} does not describe its optimization history.''')
paragraph('Importantly, the simulation runs', r'''For context, Feldbauer et al. \citep{hess-29-1183-2025} compared four calibrated one-dimensional lake-temperature models across 73 lakes. The best-performing model for each lake gave median RMSE 1.2~$^{\circ}$C and median correlation 0.98, with 95\% below 2~$^{\circ}$C. These values provide literature context rather than operational acceptance thresholds; observation coverage, dimensionality and error aggregation differ between studies.''')
paragraph('Table~\\ref{tab:performance} reports', r'''Table~\ref{tab:performance} reports the revised thermal simulation over 22 April--20 November 2024. Agreement varies with depth: RMSE is 1.12~$^{\circ}$C at 7~m, reaches 3.07~$^{\circ}$C at 18~m and is 1.57~$^{\circ}$C at 50~m. Twenty-three of the fifty depth bands have RMSE below 2~$^{\circ}$C. The final-error column records model minus observation at the last available paired time at each depth; it is not mean seasonal bias. The profile and residual plots in Appendix~\ref{app:tempvalidation} show excessive warming below the surface during stratification.''')
