# Replace repetitive discussion and conclusions, retaining the substantive limitations.
span(r'\section{Discussion}', r'%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%'+'\n'+r'\authorcontributions', r'''\section{Discussion}
\label{ch:Discussion}
The prototype connects monitoring observations with spatial modelling and source-protection analyses. Its practical contribution is the ability to use a common information workflow for inspecting conditions, exploring scenarios and identifying mobile sampling locations. It supports the information needs of a water safety plan; it does not replace treatment barriers, management procedures or the water supplier's assessment of an incident.

The thermal comparison demonstrates agreement in broad seasonal evolution and substantial discrepancies below the surface during stratification. Temperature agreement alone does not establish the velocity field driving constituent transport: different combinations of mixing, surface exchange and forcing can produce similar temperatures while producing different circulation \citep{hess-26-3651-2022}. Evaluation of state variables, process rates and system behaviour provides complementary evidence \citep{Hipsey2020assessment}. Current measurements or tracer observations would strengthen the transport assessment, but are not available for this demonstration.

The nitrogen scenario and the sampling plan are conditional demonstrations. The archived nitrogen source loading and transport convergence remain unverified, and a balance from another run does not resolve those limitations. The three reaction alternatives provide a model-spread score, not a complete uncertainty characterization. Cell resampling tests the downstream planning algorithm but cannot establish robustness to additional model alternatives. Field comparison with alternative sampling strategies is needed to determine whether the proposed planning method improves information gained per mission.

Sensor gaps and the distinction between implemented components and an unattended service also limit operational maturity. Appendix~\ref{app:workflow} reports data coverage and solver throughput. Publication latency, recovery from failures and clear indication of stale output require end-to-end testing before operational reliance.

\section{Conclusions}
\label{ch:Conclusions}
This study demonstrates a digital twin prototype integrating networked observations, an established three-dimensional model and applications for source-water protection. The contribution is the systems integration and its use for state estimation, hypothetical-release analysis and mobile-sampling planning. The numerical method and solver are established components.

For the revised thermal run, hourly temperature at 2.95~m agrees with observations at RMSE 1.73~$^{\circ}$C and Pearson correlation 0.962; the mean depth-band RMSE is 2.15~$^{\circ}$C, with larger metalimnetic errors. Chemical predictions and the sampling strategy remain unvalidated demonstrations, and the complete unattended operational loop has not been established. These limits distinguish the capabilities of the framework from the evidence provided by this case study.

The framework is potentially transferable, subject to local bathymetry, forcing, hazards, instrumentation and institutional capacity \citep{daniel2023survey}. Priorities for further work are transport validation, field evaluation of sampling strategies and testing the integrated update and publication workflow. Replication in other reservoirs would establish the conditions under which the architecture provides useful decision support.''')
